# Severity re-check — 16 records, 3 re-rated (2026-11-04)

Attached as a git note to the commit "Re-rate three incidents on the corrected facts". The commit "Check adopted VCDB
fields against their sources" (50672f7) corrected facts in the 16 records that adopt a VCDB coding. This review rated
all 16 again by methodology §7 and §8. A rating changes only where a corrected fact changes the result of the rubric;
for every other record the earlier rating stands. An attacker's unverified claim does not raise the observed severity
(§7.1, §9.1). The potential severity rates the demonstrated path as if it had completed, bounded by the documented
reach and by controls that demonstrably worked (§7.3). Calibration: the corpus's own ratings of similar records
(§8.2 anchors). Claude proposed every row; the user approved the table on 2026-11-04.

## Changed

| Record | Field | Before → after | Reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-004 | `report.observed_severity` | High → Medium | Novo Nordisk confirmed only that pseudonymised clinical-trial patient data and healthcare-provider contacts were copied, without a count. FulcrumSec's further claims are unverified: 1.3 TB, R&D secrets, AI model assets, credentials, a 264 GB leak. Similar confirmed thefts of health data with an unknown count are rated Medium: 2026-043, 053, 054. |
| 2026-004 | `report.potential_severity` | Critical → High | The completed path is the publication or sale of the copied data: a large, irreversible exposure of health-related data. The step to Critical rests only on the unverified claims. Confidence stays Medium. |
| 2026-005 | `report.observed_severity` | High → Medium | One malicious commit in one Microsoft repository; GitHub disabled 73 repositories within hours; no source reports stolen credentials (`data_disclosure` Potentially): a contained integrity compromise. 2026-046, a wave of the same Miasma campaign, is rated Medium/High. |
| 2026-005 | `report.potential_severity` | Critical → High | Every developer who opened the repository in an AI coding tool before the takedown runs the credential harvester, and the worm spreads; GitHub's detection, which demonstrably worked, limits the window to hours. Critical would need a "major supply-chain compromise", such as 2026-025 axios. |
| 2026-005 | `report.potential_severity_confidence` | High → Medium | The number of developers who opened the repository in that window is unknown. |
| 2026-041 | `report.observed_severity` | Medium → High | New facts of 50672f7: 353,844 patients (HHS breach portal), Social Security numbers among the data. High's "large-scale sensitive-data exposure", like 2026-024 (1.08M members). |
| 2026-053 | `report.observed_severity_rationale` | text only, Medium stays | The old text "Confirmed ransomware breach of patient healthcare data" contradicted `data_disclosure` Potentially. |

## Kept

- 2026-017 High/High (Claude's certainty low): Meta calls 20,225 "an upper bound of the users impacted as some of the
  accounts may have been accessed legitimately by account owners"; exploitation is confirmed and most resets were
  probably the attackers'. High's "significant credential compromise" still fits; Medium would be defensible if the
  upper bound weighs more.
- 2026-049 and 2026-053 Medium/Medium: 049's victim confirms an incident with "a limited number of individuals
  affected"; 053's actor accessed the provider's systems for three weeks (Medium: "compromise of a bounded number of
  systems").
- 2026-043 Medium/High: the confirmed ransomware and network interruption stay within Medium's "contained …
  availability loss".
- 2026-047 Medium/High: an outage of at most 16 days, Medium's "recoverable production impact".
- 2026-054 Medium/Medium: only a vendor's employees were affected, but with sensitive data; Low ("low-sensitivity")
  does not fit.
- 2026-016, 024, 029, 048, 055, 075, 076: 50672f7 changed no fact that the rating rests on.

